444	## Examples
445	
446	### 1. List today's events (searches all calendars)
447	
448	```json
449	{
450	  "name": "search_events",
451	  "arguments": {
452	    "timeMin": "2025-01-15T00:00:00Z",
453	    "timeMax": "2025-01-15T23:59:59Z"
454	  }
455	}
456	```
457	
458	> No need to call `list_calendars` first — `search_events` searches all accessible calendars by default and shows which calendar each event belongs to.
459	
460	### 2. Create event with Google Meet
461	
462	```json
463	{
464	  "name": "create_event",
465	  "arguments": {
466	    "summary": "Team Standup",
467	    "start": "2025-01-16T09:00:00+01:00",
468	    "end": "2025-01-16T09:30:00+01:00",
469	    "addGoogleMeet": true,
470	    "attendees": ["alice@example.com", "bob@example.com"]
471	  }
472	}
473	```
474	
475	### 3. Natural language event
476	
477	```json
478	{
479	  "name": "create_event",
480	  "arguments": {
481	    "text": "Coffee with Sarah next Monday at 3pm for 30 minutes"
482	  }
483	}
484	```
485	
486	### 4. Check availability before scheduling
487	
488	```json
489	{
490	  "name": "check_availability",
491	  "arguments": {
492	    "timeMin": "2025-01-16T09:00:00Z",
493	    "timeMax": "2025-01-16T18:00:00Z"
494	  }
495	}
496	```
